 "pack": "emoji",
 "notes": "Coverage: 100 unique movies, 1930s through 2020s, spanning animation, horror, sci-fi, musicals, crime, action, romance, and holiday films. Difficulty skews easy by design: roughly 40 at difficulty 1, 45 at difficulty 2, 15 at difficulty 3, which satisfies the \"at least 70 solvable at difficulty 1-2\" rule with margin; if you want a harder pack, promote borderline 2s (Psycho, Truman Show, Godfather) to 3 rather than adding obscure films. Cross-platform rendering flags to verify at import: (1) ZWJ sequences 👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 👨‍👦 👨‍🍳 👨‍🔬 👨‍🚀 👩‍🚀 👩‍🏫 👮‍♀️ 💇‍♀️ 🧙‍♂️ 🧜‍♀️ 👱‍♀️ and 🏴‍☠️ can split into component glyphs on older Android/Windows builds; every sequence still reads acceptably if split, but test the family emoji (Sound of Music, Encanto, Incredibles, Shining) on your minimum target OS. (2) 🧊 (Titanic) and 🪓 (The Shining) are Emoji 12.0 (2019); supported everywhere modern but they are the newest code points used, swap 🧊 for ❄️ and 🪓 for 🔪 if you must support pre-2020 devices. (3) 🗿 (Moana) and 🏺 (Ghost) render with noticeably different art across platforms but are universally present. No keycap numbers, no letter emoji, no regional-indicator flags were used. Verify exact official titles at import against your TMDB data, especially \"E.T. the Extra-Terrestrial\", \"WALL-E\", \"Se7en\" (TMDB lists it as \"Se7en\"), \"Star Wars\" (1977 entry, not \"A New Hope\" on some databases), and \"How the Grinch Stole Christmas\" (2000 live action, distinct from the 2018 \"The Grinch\"). Decoys are all real films chosen to be plausible for the same emoji; two decoys (Monsters University, Hannibal) are franchise siblings of the answer, which is intentional but worth a gameplay check.",
